{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>A man, his mother, etc.</b> The reason for the juxtaposition of this commandment to the commandment of Kedoshim is to allude also to a safeguard against forbidden relations. It is along the lines of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 36b), that they expounded the verse ״וַיָּפֹזּוּ זְרֹעֵי יָדָיו מִידֵי אֲבִיר יַעֲקֹב״ etc., for when the wife of Potiphar pressed Joseph and his inclination overcame him, the image of his father appeared to him and said to him, etc.; immediately ״וַיָּפֹזּוּ זְרֹעֵי״ etc. And I have heard from the men of truth (Orchot Tzaddikim, ch. 2) that the image of the father strengthens the power of holiness in the son and restrains him from entering into abomination — and this is the verse's saying, adjacent to the commandment concerning forbidden relations, <b>his father and his mother you shall fear.</b> Therefore one whose inclination overcomes him should picture his parents between his eyes, and it will be for him a restoration of soul." "<b>Also</b> it alludes that when he commits the transgression, he dishonors his father's honor; therefore the El commands, adjacent to the commandment concerning forbidden relations, the commandment of fear of father and mother. From this you learn that if he uncovered forbidden relations, he nullified the commandment of fear of father and mother, for he caused them contempt, such that they will say: Cursed be the one who fathered this one, etc. And along this line the mother is placed before the father, for she bears the greater shame, as it says (Proverbs 10:1): ״וּבֵן כְּסִיל תּוּגַת אִמּוֹ״." "And along this line we have found a correct reason also for the juxtaposition of <b>and et My Sabbaths you shall keep</b>. It is along the lines of what they said of blessed memory in the Zohar (vol. 2, p. 207; vol. 3, p. 1) that seven righteous men correspond to the seven days of the week. And they said in Zohar Chadash (beginning of parashat Toldot) that the day of Shabbat corresponds to Joseph the righteous, and he is the secret of peace; therefore we say Shabbat Shalom and we bless ״the One who spreads the sukka of peace,״ and it is the aspect of yesod of all, and Joseph, on account of his righteousness in guarding the sign of the holy covenant from approaching impurity, inherited this aspect. That is what is said here <b>and et My Sabbaths</b> — two Sabbaths: one is the guarding of oneself from contaminating the sign of the holy covenant, and one is the guarding of Shabbat, and both are one aspect. Therefore both are called signs: concerning Shabbat it says (Exodus 31:13) ״אוֹת הִיא בֵּינִי״ etc., and concerning circumcision it says (Genesis 17:11) ״וְהָיָה לְאוֹת״. And know that the two aspects are two in terms of the practical dimension, but in terms of the spiritual influence generated by them it is one aspect, and one who keeps one of them will draw the rectification and the bonding of his soul in both commandments — therefore it says <b>and et My Sabbaths.</b>" "<b>And his father you shall fear.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said that in one place the verse placed the father before the mother, as it is written (Exodus 20:12): ״כַּבֵּד אֶת אָבִיךָ וְאֶת אִמֶּךָ״ etc., and in another place it placed the mother first, etc. — to say that both are equal. But the Sages said the father precedes the mother. Also the baraita, brought in Kiddushin fol. 31 — Rabbi expounded, and this is his language: It is revealed and known before the One who spoke and the world came to be, etc., that a son honors his mother, etc.; therefore He placed the honor of the father first, etc. And it is revealed and known, etc., that the son fears his father, etc.; therefore HaShem placed the fear of the mother first, etc. — end of quote. And it is difficult for me regarding both expoundings, for when we examine the matter, in both verses the verse reckoned the father more than the mother: in the verse ״כַּבֵּד״ etc. it attached the father to [the word] honor, in saying ״כַּבֵּד אֶת אָבִיךָ״; also in this verse it attached the father to [the word] fear, in saying ״וְאָבִיו תִּירָאוּ״. And along this line we find that our Sages of blessed memory expounded in several places. And it is true, if there were no halakhic ruling involved, I could explain the verse according to our view, for we are already given license to explain the verse even if it follows a different order than the words of the earlier ones, provided the halakhic ruling does not change. But in what is before us the ruling would change — if she was divorced: for according to what we explained, that the Torah placed the father before the mother in both verses, if his father says ״give me water to drink״ and his mother says ״give me water to drink״ — [the question is] whether to precede his father or his mother. But according to our Sages who said they are equal, except that since he and his mother are both obligated in the honor of his father, if she was divorced and that reason does not apply — he may choose to precede his father, he may choose to precede his mother. And so they said there in Kiddushin regarding a certain widow's son who asked Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua, and this is their language: If she was divorced, what [is the law]? He said to him: Put water for them in a pitcher, etc. — end of quote. According to Rabbi Eliezer's view, they are precisely equal." "Or perhaps, for according to what they expounded of blessed memory there in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״אִישׁ״ — I have only a man; from where [do I include] a woman? Scripture teaches ״תִּירָאוּ״. If so, why is ״אִישׁ״ stated? Because the man has [the matter] in his hand [i.e., within his power]; a woman does not have it in her hand to act — end of quote. Behold, according to this baraita, ״אִישׁ״ is stated to exclude a woman regarding the matter of honor. Say then that regarding the matter of honor, ״אִישׁ אִמּוֹ״ etc. is stated, but regarding the mother it is not the same matter, and accordingly there is room to prioritize the mother in the aspect of honor. And all this is to settle the baraita of Torat Kohanim; but regarding the expounding of Rabbi, who expounded that the verse placed the mother before the father in the aspect of fear, etc. (Kiddushin 31a), it is still difficult. Or perhaps, since this is only a matter of aggadah and no halakhic ruling emerges from it, the expounding may stand as it is — for after all, the verse did place the mother before the father, for it should have said ״אִישׁ יִירָא אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו״, and even though the verse was compelled to speak in this order, to say ״אִישׁ״ to exclude a woman from the obligation of honor and to say ״תִּירָאוּ״ to include [women] in the aspect of fear, nevertheless after all the order of the mother before the father stands, and the aggadah hangs on whatever allusion there may be." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that the Tannaim did not hold that because Scripture juxtaposed the mention of awe to the father, he should thereby be greater than the mother — for the verse was constrained in its wording to speak in this order, ״אִישׁ … תִּירָאוּ״, in order to derive a limitation and an expansion regarding women. Since this is so, we may say with full certainty that the one mentioned earlier in the verse is primary, and He placed ״תִּירָאוּ״ at the end not to juxtapose the father to it so that he would be superior to the mother, but rather to derive from it an expansion obligating the woman in the awe of parents." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that the reason He used singular language and concluded with plural language is to say that when the son is in awe of his father, this also causes his own son to be in awe of him. If you wish, say it accords with what we stated earlier in other places (Genesis 49:3), that the roots of holiness and their counterparts found in the begetter produce their like in those who are born; and if you wish, say it is from the way of moral instruction, that when the son sees his father slighting the honor of his own father, the garment of awe is stripped from him as well, and when a person is in awe of his father etc., the son takes lesson. Moreover, it implants in the son's soul the aspect of the effect of good, so that he is found to fulfill two awes — and this is the verse's saying <b>אִישׁ</b> in singular language, <b>תִּירָאוּ</b> in plural language, for through one awe two are in awe. And this is their saying (Avot 4:2), מִצְוָה גּוֹרֶרֶת מִצְוָה — one mitzvah drags along another mitzvah." "<b>Or</b> it may say along the lines of what the Tur ruled in siman 240, and this is his language: ״And it appears to me that since he is a wicked man, one is not obligated to honor him, as they say (Bava Kamma 94b) in the case of one who left them a stolen cow — they are obligated to return it because of their father's honor, and the Gemara asks: but he is not one who acts as 'your people' [i.e., not observant], and they are not obligated to honor him? And they answer: when he repented. We see that anyone who has not repented, they are not obligated to honor him — end of quotation.״ The Rav [the Tur] holds that one may not presume him to have repented unless we saw him do so specifically. And this is the verse's saying <b>אִישׁ אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו</b> — meaning, a man who conducts himself with his mother and father in the manner of [honoring] father and mother — <b>תִּירָאוּ</b> — you shall be in awe of this one; but one who slights the honor of his father and mother is exempt from awe. And the word ״אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו״ refers back to the man who begets and extends also to ״תִּירָאוּ״, to tell upon whom the commandment of awe falls. Understand this." "And also according to what I wrote in my work Pri To'ar, where we upheld the words of the Rambam who wrote that from the unqualified presumption, a person is presumed to have repented — and his proof is from that which they said in the Gemara (Kiddushin 49b), in the case of one who says to a woman, ״הֲרֵי אַתְּ מְקֻדֶּשֶׁת לִי עַל מְנָת שֶׁאֲנִי צַדִּיק גָּמוּר״ etc., where we say ״he has repented״ even though we did not see him do so etc. — and we rejected the Tur's proof from ״one who left them their father's stolen cow,״ for that case is different, for his deeds prove that he did not repent, since the cow of his sin still stands alive even after his death; but in any case where there is no such proof, from the unqualified presumption he stands with the presumption of having repented — even according to this, our words hold in a case where we see clearly that he does not honor them, and there is no room to presume repentance, and it is similar to one who leaves a stolen cow. Such a man — his honor and his awe are stripped from his children in accordance with his own conduct." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of their saying, of blessed memory, in that story of that man etc. [who was commanded by his father not to speak between putting down the hat and lighting the lamp, and his wife came and spoke to him], and he heard [her] but silenced himself, because of ״לִפְנֵי עִוֵּר לֹא תִתֵּן מִכְשׁוֹל״ — and this is the verse's saying here ״תִּירָאוּ״ in plural language: even though He commanded only one person, as it is written ״אִישׁ״, nevertheless the mitzvah falls upon the one who is to be feared and upon the one who fears — lest he cause the one commanded not to be in awe, lest he bring him to stumble." "<b>וְאֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ, etc.</b> Our Sages said (Bava Metzia 32b): From where do we learn that if his father said to him, ״Desecrate the Sabbath,״ etc., that he must not obey him? For it is written ״אִישׁ אִמּוֹ, etc., וְאֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי, etc., אֲנִי ה׳״ — that you and your father are both obligated in My honor — end quote. Now, as for the reason they gave, ״you and your father, etc.,״ which they derived from the statement ״אֲנִי ה׳,״ it is understood that this same rule would apply to all the commandments of the Torah, even though they are not as stringent as the Sabbath, such as returning a lost object or defilement from the dead. If so, why did He juxtapose specifically the commandment of the Sabbath, which is stringent? He should have juxtaposed some other, lighter commandment. Also, for this He would not have needed to give the reason ״אֲנִי ה׳״ to derive from it that the same applies to other lighter commandments." "And it may be explained based on what the Rambam wrote in chapter 5 of Hilkhot Mamrim, and this is his language: ״If his father and mother were completely wicked, etc., it is forbidden for him to strike them, etc.״ — end quote. And all the later authorities were precise that specifically striking them is forbidden, but he is not obligated in their honor and their awe. And I raised [the matter] in my work Rosh Yosef — may his memory be for a blessing — in reconciling the words of the Rambam with what he wrote in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Mamrim, that one is obligated to honor them, for he wrote that only regarding one who has committed some transgressions occasionally out of desire, whereas here he speaks of one who is established as a completely wicked person. Therefore he was precise in his language, ״completely wicked,״ and did not suffice with saying ״wicked.״ And I believe that ״completely wicked״ means one who transgresses all the transgressions that come to hand — regarding such a one there is no obligation upon the son to fear them and to honor them. And this is the intent of the verse here: <b>אִישׁ אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו תִּירָאוּ</b>, and it is a condition [of the matter]: <b>וְאֶת שַׁבְּתֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ</b> — all of you, fathers and sons. And examine from this: if the fathers do not keep the Sabbaths of HaShem, then the commandment of awe for them falls away, for the Sabbath is weighed against the entire Torah (Shemot Rabbah 25:12), and such a one is called completely wicked." "<b>Further,</b> the entire verse may be interpreted by way of remez, while also raising [a question]: why did He say <b>שַׁבְּתוֹתַי</b> in the plural and not in the singular as is customary throughout the Torah? And our Sages said (Shabbat 69b) that it comes to assign one [act of] guarding to many Sabbaths, and the intent of this is that if one performed many labors on many Sabbaths in a single state of unawareness of the Sabbath, he is liable only for one sin-offering. And it appears by way of what they said in the Zohar (vol. 2, 2a) that <b>שַׁבְּתוֹתַי</b> alludes to the night and to the day, and this is its language: ״This is Shabbata and the eve of Shabbata, for the night should be sanctified like the day.״ For inasmuch as the aspect of night is not equal to the sanctity of the day — for the night is the aspect of light that is associated with the feminine, and it is the aspect of ״שָׁמוֹר (Keep)״; and the day is the aspect of supernal light, associated with the masculine, which flows into the light of the night as is known to those who know grace — therefore it is associated with ״זָכוֹר (Remember).״ And therefore the Zohar says (vol. 2, 88a) that the meal of the night is the meal of the holy Apple Orchard and the meal of the day is the meal of the Holy Ancient One, the Father of Mercy, and therefore the sanctification of the day is called by the Sages (Pesachim 106a) Kiddusha Rabba. And I also heard and saw it written (at the end of the Shulchan Aruch of the Ari z״l) of a certain incident in the days of Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, that a certain sectarian arose and wrote with proofs that on the day of the Sabbath the day should precede and only afterward the night. And the matter was revealed to the Rav by means of a miracle, and he confuted his counsels, etc., as is expounded from his book (Iggeret Shabbat). And against the opinion of this sectarian the verse spoke and said <b>אִישׁ אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו תִּירָאוּ</b> — it placed mother before father, and it juxtaposed to this commandment the commandment of the Sabbath and said <b>וְאֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי</b>, which are the night and the day, etc., to say that even in them the aspect of the mother — which is the night — should precede the aspect of the father — which is the day: for just as the order of parents [is mother then father], so the order of Sabbaths — the eve of Shabbata and Shabbata — and not according to the opinion of the aforementioned sectarians."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {12 "Resolved the abbreviation ד״ק as דַּוְקָא (״specifically״) rather than דִּיקְדּוּק (״a fine point״), following the context where the later authorities are being cited for a precise legal distinction."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:05.620654431Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}